$DIR/main.p:32:8: cannot pass &ev to fmt.Errorf, which keeps it after returning
    &ev points into ev. ev is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:30).
    fmt.Errorf stores a pointer loaded from args[i] in memory that outlives the
    call (errorf.p:45), so &ev would outlive ev.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Errorf should keep its own copy of ev, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[E] from new, a shared[E],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:8: cannot pass &in to fmt.Errorf, which keeps it after returning
    &in points into in. in is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:28).
    fmt.Errorf passes a pointer reachable from args to (*printer).doPrintf
    (errorf.p:37), which passes a pointer reachable from args to
    (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:880), which passes a pointer reachable from arg
    to a formatting method of what it walks (which may call
    (synced[pluto/os.PathError]).Error) (fmt.p:671), which passes a pointer
    reachable from e to e.Err.Error (which may call os.(*PathError).Error)
    (os_linux.p:623), which passes a pointer reachable from e to e.Err.Error
    (which may call os.(*PathError).Error) (os_linux.p:623), which passes a
    pointer reachable from e to e.Err.Error (which may call main.(*E).Error)
    (os_linux.p:623), which stores a pointer loaded from e.p in package variable
    keep (main.p:19), so &in would outlive in.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Errorf should keep its own copy of in, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[Inner] from new, a
    shared[Inner], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
